[[:db/retract 17592186092600 :question/answer-fields 17592186089771] [:db/retract 17592186092602 :question/answer-fields 17592186089783] {:db/id 17592186092338 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a66f5e5d-805e-5011-9534-19a9e92d3193" "question-3574e15c-1d81-5d76-a394-37484fa9dfbb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186091944 :question/problem "![](images/45/45a423930fb07c365f10cdfbdeb3d45ef337078fa77e1b0699388088438d4c70.png)\n\n \n\nWhat is the subset $A$ of $U=\\{ 1,2,3,4,5 \\}$ whose indicator function $\\boldsymbol{1}_A(x)$ is shown above?" :question/worked-solution "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A \\subseteq X$ is the function\n![](images/15/159dcc0eb98804f6d4d281603de45cf00922023ce1d427ff73a7188bea38e40d.svg)\n\nIn the diagram above, only the elements $1,2,$ and $5$ are mapped to $1.$ Therefore, the indicator function in the diagram above corresponds to the set $A=\\{ 1,2,5 \\}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092339 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-92b8a61e-f7a9-5e1e-adba-90e2fa6c6243" "question-6c60efa7-9f70-50c9-8725-0334784ed791" "question-a8a05322-998a-5d01-8b89-68a8bc93dd0f" "question-09ca95e9-e7fd-5d14-80c3-ab64bc50a23d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186091945 :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{1}_{A}(-1) = 0, \\qquad \\mathbf{1}_{B}(-1) = 1\n$$\n\nGiven the values of the indicator functions of the sets $A$ and $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{1}_{A \\,\\cup\\, B}\\,(-1) = 1$\n\n- $\\mathbf{1}_{A \\,\\cup\\, B}\\,(-1) = 0$\n\n- $-1 \\in A \\cup B$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overline{A},$ $A \\cap B,$ and $A \\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(-1) & =max{{1}_{A}(-1),{1}_{B}(-1)} \\\\  & =max{0,1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\mathbf{1}_{A \\,\\cup\\, B}\\,(-1) = 1,$ we conclude that $-1$ belongs to $A \\cup B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-a66f5e5d-805e-5011-9534-19a9e92d3193" :question/id #uuid "a66f5e5d-805e-5011-9534-19a9e92d3193" :question/math-academy-id "q-174359" :question/problem "![](images/7c/7c60072882a6effae61da543bfddff1053ab0bcc924d12c49f5acd697b0871c4.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the subset $A$ of $U={a,b,c,d,e}$ whose indicator function ${1}_{A}(x)$ is shown above?" :question/worked-solution "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A⊆X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)={\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }x\\in A, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }x\\notin A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the diagram above, only the elements $b,c$ and $e$ are mapped to $1.$ Therefore, the indicator function in the diagram above corresponds to the set $A={b,c,e}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-72880716-881b-572f-a482-436f0d9e99ca"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-72880716-881b-572f-a482-436f0d9e99ca" :answer-field/id #uuid "72880716-881b-572f-a482-436f0d9e99ca" :answer-fie